- How would you adapt it if dictionary lookups are expensive and you need pruning?
- How would you extend to handle negative scores or required minimum total?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to translate dependency constraints into a topological order and compute earliest finish times via DP. It also touches complexity and edge cases (cycles).

## Key Concepts

- Topological sort (Kahn or DFS)
- DAG longest path / critical path
- Dynamic programming on DAG
- Time and space complexity

## Code Example

```javascript
function computeProject(durations, edges) {
  const n = durations.length;
  const adj = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);
  for (const [u, v] of edges) { adj[u].push(v); indeg[v]++; }
  const est = Array(n).fill(0);
  const finish = Array(n).fill(0);
  const q = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (indeg[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  let idx = 0;
  while (idx < q.length) {
    const u = q[idx++];
    finish[u] = est[u] + durations[u];
    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      est[v] = Math.max(est[v], finish[u]);
      if (--indeg[v] === 0) q.push(v);
    }
  }
  const project = Math.max(...finish);
  return { finish, project };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect cycles if the DAG assumption fails? 
- How would you adapt for tasks with minimum and maximum parallelism constraints?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to combine shortest-path computation, path counting, and path reconstruction into a cohesive solution. It also evaluates handling of multiple equal-cost routes and lexicographic tie-breaking.

## Key Concepts
- Dijkstra's algorithm for O(m log n) shortest paths
- Shortest-path DAG construction
- Topological ordering by distance values
- DP for counting distinct shortest paths
- Lexicographic path reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestStats(n, edges, s, t) {
  const adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  for (const [u, v, w] of edges) {
    adj[u].push([v, w]);
  }

  const dist = new Array(n).fill(Infinity);
  const pq = [[0, s]];
  dist[s] = 0;

  while (pq.length) {
    const [d, u] = pq.pop();
    if (d > dist[u]) continue;
    for (const [v, w] of adj[u]) {
      if (dist[u] + w < dist[v]) {
        dist[v] = dist[u] + w;
        pq.push([dist[v], v]);
      }
    }
  }

  const dag = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  for (let u = 0; u < n; u++) {
    for (const [v, w] of adj[u]) {
      if (dist[u] + w === dist[v]) {
        dag[u].push(v);
      }
    }
  }

  const ways = new Array(n).fill(0);
  ways[s] = 1;
  const order = Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => i)
    .sort((a, b) => dist[a] - dist[b]);

  for (const u of order) {
    for (const v of dag[u]) {
      ways[v] = (ways[v] + ways[u]) % 1000000007;
    }
  }

  const path = [];
  let curr = t;
  while (curr !== null) {
    path.unshift(curr);
    const preds = dag
      .map((list, u) => list.includes(curr) ? u : -1)
      .filter(u => u >= 0 && dist[curr] === dist[u] + 
        adj[u].find(([v]) => v === curr)?.[1]);
    preds.sort((a, b) => a - b);
    curr = preds.length > 0 ? preds[0] : null;
    if (curr === s) {
      path.unshift(s);
      break;
    }
  }

  return { distance: dist[t], ways: ways[t], path };
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- Dijkstra: O(m log n)
- DAG construction: O(m)
- DP counting: O(m)
- Path reconstruction: O(n + m)
- Overall: O(m log n)

## Why This Is Asked

This problem tests interval DP and both memoization and tabulation approaches. It asks for precise state definitions, correct recurrence when ends match, and careful base cases. It also probes understanding of time/space trade-offs for cubic-time DP.

## Key Concepts

- DP on substrings
- Memoization vs tabulation
- Interval DP with matching ends

## Code Example

```javascript
function minPalRemoval(s){
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let i=n-1;i>=0;i--){
    dp[i][i] = 1;
    for(let j=i+1;j<n;j++){
      dp[i][j] = 1 + dp[i+1][j];
      for(let k=i+1;k<=j;k++){
        if(s[i] === s[k]){
          const left = (k-1 >= i+1) ? dp[i+1][k-1] : 0;
          const right = (k+1 <= j) ? dp[k+1][j] : 0;
          dp[i][j] = Math.min(dp[i][j], left + right);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return n ? dp[0][n-1] : 0;
}
```

