- How would you adapt to detect cycles and report the cycle path?
- How would you extend to handle parallel resources or varying durations per edge?

## Why This Is Asked

This problem tests practical DP skills: counting subsets, managing zeros, and comparing memoization vs tabulation.

## Key Concepts

- Subset sum counting
- Top-down memoization
- Bottom-up tabulation
- Modulo arithmetic and zero handling

## Code Example

```javascript
function countSubsetsTopDown(nums, target) {
  const n = nums.length;
  const memo = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Map());
  function dfs(i, t){
    if (t === 0) return 1;
    if (i === n) return 0;
    const key = t;
    if (memo[i].has(key)) return memo[i].get(key);
    let res = dfs(i+1, t);
    if (t >= nums[i]) res = (res + dfs(i+1, t - nums[i]));
    memo[i].set(key, res);
    return res;
  }
  return dfs(0, target);
}
function countSubsetsBottomUp(nums, target){
  const MOD = 1000000007;
  const dp = new Array(target+1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  for (const v of nums){
    for (let w = target; w >= v; w--){
      dp[w] = (dp[w] + dp[w - v]) % MOD;
    }
  }
  return dp[target];
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to handle negative numbers or count modulo large M?
- How would you modify to return counts for multiple target sums simultaneously?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests fundamental graph algorithms, DAG analysis, and dynamic programming techniques commonly used in project scheduling and dependency management systems.

## Key Concepts
- **Topological Sorting**: Linear ordering respecting task dependencies
- **Dynamic Programming on DAGs**: Computing optimal values through predecessor relationships
- **Earliest Finish Times**: Maximum of predecessor completion plus task duration
- **Makespan Calculation**: Overall project completion time
- **Complexity**: O(N + E) time and space

## Code Example
```javascript
function makespan(n, durations, edges) {
  // Build adjacency list and compute indegrees
  const adj = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);

  for (const [u, v] of edges) {
    adj[u].push(v);
    indeg[v]++;
  }

  // Initialize earliest finish times
  const earliestFinish = durations.slice();
  const queue = [];

  // Start with source nodes (indegree = 0)
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (indeg[i] === 0) queue.push(i);
  }

  // Process nodes in topological order
  let head = 0;
  while (head < queue.length) {
    const u = queue[head++];

    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      // Update earliest finish for successor
      earliestFinish[v] = Math.max(
        earliestFinish[v],
        earliestFinish[u] + durations[v]
      );

      indeg[v]--;
      if (indeg[v] === 0) queue.push(v);
    }
  }

  // Return makespan (maximum earliest finish time)
  return Math.max(...earliestFinish);
}
```

## Algorithm Insight
The topological approach ensures each node is processed only after all its predecessors are complete, allowing accurate computation of earliest start times and efficient makespan calculation.

## Why This Is Asked
This probes translating a string problem into substring subproblems and requires a clear bottom-up DP setup.

## Key Concepts
- Substring DP: dp[i][j] for i..j
- Base cases: i >= j -> 0 insertions
- Bottom-up vs memoization: space/time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function minInsertions(s){
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let i = n-2; i >= 0; i--){
    for(let j = i+1; j < n; j++){
      if(s[i] === s[j]) dp[i][j] = dp[i+1][j-1];
      else dp[i][j] = 1 + Math.min(dp[i+1][j], dp[i][j-1]);
    }
  }
  return n ? dp[0][n-1] : 0;
}
```

